## What to return

**Which of two answers applies is a question about the whole document, never about one line of
it.** A document that restates or indexes material stated authoritatively elsewhere states no
result of its own: a notation table, a glossary, a reference list, a section preamble. Its lines
read as assertions, and each one recalls a result some other document states first — a table of
symbols is such a document however many of its rows would make sentences.

Otherwise the document states results of its own, and every one of them is one block. Record what
the document states rather than only what it proves formally: a result stated in prose is a
result.

**You name where a result begins; the tool works out how far it runs.** A result runs from the
sentence you name to the next sentence you name in the same paragraph, or to the end of that
paragraph. So name the sentence each result begins at; how much of the document a result covers
is not something the answer carries.

Each block carries three lines:

- **`quote:`** the opening of the sentence the result begins at, copied from the body above in the
  document's own words — around ten words, enough that no other sentence opens the same way.
  Leave the label and its colon out of it: they are this rendering's and not the document's. The
  quote is how the tool finds the sentence, and never what it records.
- **`label:`** the label of that same sentence. The tool resolves the quote itself and reads this
  as a cross-check, so a quote and a label naming two different sentences is an answer it cannot
  use.
- **`title:`** yours to write: one line naming the result. It is a title and never a heading — no
  leading `#`, no numbering, no markup — and no two blocks from this document may share one.

A structural label standing on its own — `Proof.`, `Definition 3.` — is not a sentence a result
begins at.

One block per result, its two markers on lines of their own. Nothing numbers them and nothing in
one refers to another:

    @!claim-open!@
    quote: The bifurcation is supercritical whenever $\sigma < 1$
    label: S12
    title: Supercriticality of the bifurcation below the critical rate
    @!claim-close!@
    @!claim-open!@
    quote: Every trajectory of the system remains bounded for all time
    label: S19
    title: Boundedness of every trajectory
    @!claim-close!@

A document stating no result of its own returns no block of that kind and exactly one of this
kind, holding one sentence:

    @!no-claim-open!@
    This section fixes the notation the rest of the volume uses and states no result of its own.
    @!no-claim-close!@

That sentence is permanent: it is written into this document's frontmatter and is what a later
reader is told about why this document carries no result. Say what the document is and what that
makes of its lines. Write it yourself, and do not hand back a sentence already sitting in this
document's frontmatter — that is the sentence which made this document a question.

Returning both kinds of block, or neither, is not an answer.

